    "raw_fragment_sha256": "1a4a38e454abad55d1fb3cbbb74b352cc0d3f0747f1c2a2541acf7d0442358d8",
    "text_with_math_alttext": "Proof of the well-posedness assertion in Proposition 3.1. Fix $y,\\widetilde{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$. In the notation of (3.4), the bounce rate (3.9) reads $\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)=\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,[p^{\\top}h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)]_{+}\\,.$ The deterministic harmonic flow, namely the solution (4.5) of (3.8), and the bounce map are $\\displaystyle\\Phi_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)$ $\\displaystyle\\coloneqq\\bigl(c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}+(x-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}})\\cos t+\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,p\\sin t,\\;-\\eta^{-1/2}(x-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}})\\sin t+p\\cos t\\bigr)\\,,$ $\\displaystyle\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)$ $\\displaystyle\\coloneqq(x,R_{h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)}p)\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptR_{0}\\coloneqq I\\,.$ The bounce map is an involution. Although there is a possible discontinuity at $h_{\\widetilde{x}}(x)=0$, this creates no ambiguity because the event rate vanishes there. Let $(E_{n})_{n\\geq 1}$ be independent $\\operatorname{Exp}(1)$ random variables. Starting from $\\zeta_{0}\\coloneqq(x_{0},p_{0})$ at time $T_{0}\\coloneqq 0$, define recursively $\\displaystyle S_{n+1}$ $\\displaystyle\\coloneqq\\inf\\Bigl\\{u\\geq 0:\\int_{0}^{u}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\Phi_{s}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{T_{n}}))\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\geq E_{n+1}\\Bigr\\}\\,,$ (A.1) $\\displaystyle T_{n+1}$ $\\displaystyle\\coloneqq T_{n}+S_{n+1}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\zeta_{T_{n+1}}\\coloneqq\\mathsf{S}_{\\widetilde{x}}\\bigl(\\Phi_{S_{n+1}}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{T_{n}})\\bigr)\\,,$ (A.2) when $S_{n+1}<\\infty$. If the set is empty, set $T_{n+1}=S_{n+1}=\\infty$ and stop the recursion. Between jumps, set $\\zeta_{T_{n}+t}=\\Phi_{t}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{T_{n}})$ for $0\\leq t<S_{n+1}$. To prove non-explosion, define the shifted harmonic energy $H_{y,\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)\\coloneqq\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\bigl\\{\\eta^{-1}\\,\\|x-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\|^{2}+\\|p\\|^{2}\\bigr\\}\\,.$ The flow and each bounce preserve this energy. Hence, with $\\mathcal{E}\\coloneqq H_{y,\\widetilde{x}}(x_{0},p_{0})$, every state produced by the recursion satisfies $\\|p\\|\\leq\\sqrt{2\\mathcal{E}}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\|x-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}\\|\\leq\\sqrt{2\\eta\\mathcal{E}}\\,.$ Because $\\nabla V$ is $\\beta$-Lipschitz, $\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(x,p)\\leq\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})$ throughout the path, where $\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})\\coloneqq\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,\\beta\\sqrt{2\\mathcal{E}}\\,\\bigl\\{\\sqrt{2\\eta\\mathcal{E}}+\\|c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}-\\widetilde{x}\\|\\bigr\\}\\,.$ Hence, $\\int_{0}^{u}\\lambda_{\\widetilde{x}}(\\Phi_{s}^{y,\\widetilde{x}}(\\zeta_{T_{n}}))\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\leq\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})\\,u\\,.$ If $\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})=0$, the event rate vanishes and there are no jumps. Otherwise, (A.1) implies, for every finite waiting time, $S_{n+1}\\geq\\frac{E_{n+1}}{\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})}\\,.$ If the recursion does not terminate, it follows that $T_{n}\\geq\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^{n}E_{j}}{\\bar{\\lambda}_{\\mathcal{E}}(y,\\widetilde{x})}\\longrightarrow\\infty$ almost surely by the strong law of large numbers. Thus, the jump times cannot accumulate, and the recursive construction defines a unique process for all $t\\geq 0$. Finally, the memoryless property of the exponential clocks gives the time-homogeneous Markov property. This is precisely the integrated hazard construction of Davis [12, Section 2]. ∎",
    "math_alttexts": [
