      "utf8_byte_end_exclusive": 95317,
      "raw_fragment_sha256": "1508bd1c44639f750bd08a110c75407309f1515e3b3e87e657bb65ba7fc9f8c7",
      "text_with_math_alttext": "2.1 The standard Bouncy Particle Sampler We first recall the usual Bouncy Particle Sampler (BPS) for readers unfamiliar with the construction [5]. It is a piecewise-deterministic Markov process on phase space $(x,p)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, where $p$ denotes velocity (equivalently, momentum for unit mass). Its invariant distribution is $\\mu\\otimes\\varphi$, where $\\varphi=\\mathcal{N}(0,I)$. Between events, the particle moves along a straight line with constant $p$: $\\dot{x}_{t}=p_{t}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\dot{p}_{t}=0\\,.$ (2.1) At state $(x,p)$, a bounce occurs at rate $\\lambda_{\\rm BPS}(x,p)\\coloneqq[p^{\\top}\\nabla V(x)]_{+}\\,.$ (2.2) Equivalently, conditional on the current state and in the absence of a velocity refresh, the next bounce time $\\tau_{\\rm b}$ satisfies $\\mathbb{P}(\\tau_{\\rm b}>t\\mid x,p)=\\exp\\Bigl\\{-\\int_{0}^{t}[p^{\\top}\\nabla V(x+sp)]_{+}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\Bigr\\}\\,.$ (2.3) At a bounce, the position is unchanged and the velocity is specularly reflected against the gradient. For non-zero $h\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, set $R_{h}\\coloneqq I-2\\,\\frac{hh^{\\top}}{\\|h\\|^{2}}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptR_{0}\\coloneqq I\\,.$ (2.4) The bounce update is $p\\leftarrow R_{\\nabla V(x)}p$. It preserves $\\|p\\|$, leaves the component tangent to a level set unchanged, and reverses the normal component, since $(R_{h}p)^{\\top}h=-p^{\\top}h$. Thus, the particle travels freely while moving down the potential and bounces, at a rate equal to its instantaneous uphill directional derivative, when it moves up the potential. Proximal BPS reuses this bounce mechanism, applied to a localized conditional target; the construction is given in Section 3. The standard bouncy particle sampler also uses independent velocity refreshes to ensure ergodicity, at which $p$ is redrawn from $\\varphi$. Writing $(\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}f)(x,p)\\coloneqq\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(x,p^{\\prime})\\,\\varphi(\\mathrm{d}p^{\\prime})\\,,$ the full generator of BPS is $\\begin{split}(\\mathsf{L}_{\\rm BPS}f)(x,p)&=p^{\\top}\\nabla_{x}f(x,p)+[p^{\\top}\\nabla V(x)]_{+}\\{f(x,R_{\\nabla V(x)}p)-f(x,p)\\}+\\gamma(\\mathsf{\\Pi}_{p}f-f)(x,p)\\,.\\end{split}$ (2.5) For every refresh rate $\\gamma\\geq 0$, the process with generator (2.5) leaves $\\mu\\otimes\\varphi$ invariant [5], and its position marginal is the desired target $\\mu$. A standard way to simulate the inhomogeneous event clock (2.3) is Poisson thinning [18]. Suppose that, along the current deterministic segment, the rate $\\lambda(\\cdot)$ is bounded above by a constant $\\bar{\\lambda}$. Generate candidate times from a homogeneous Poisson process of rate $\\bar{\\lambda}$ and accept a candidate at time $t$ with probability $\\lambda(t)/\\bar{\\lambda}$; a rejected candidate causes no jump, and the deterministic trajectory continues. The accepted times then have rate $\\lambda(\\cdot)$, so this procedure simulates the event clock exactly. Note that only at each candidate time, one needs to query the potential gradients. If no convenient dominating bound is available, accepting instead with probability $\\min\\{1,\\lambda(t)/\\bar{\\lambda}\\}$ exactly simulates the truncated rate $\\min\\{\\lambda(t),\\bar{\\lambda}\\}$; the only approximation is then the replacement of the original rate by its truncation. This is used in Section 4.2.",
      "math_alttexts": [
        "(x,p)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{d}",
        "p",
        "\\mu\\otimes\\varphi",
        "\\varphi=\\mathcal{N}(0,I)",
        "p",
        "\\dot{x}_{t}=p_{t}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003pt\\dot{p}_{t}=0\\,.",
        "(x,p)",
        "\\lambda_{\\rm BPS}(x,p)\\coloneqq[p^{\\top}\\nabla V(x)]_{+}\\,.",
        "\\tau_{\\rm b}",
        "\\mathbb{P}(\\tau_{\\rm b}>t\\mid x,p)=\\exp\\Bigl\\{-\\int_{0}^{t}[p^{\\top}\\nabla V(x+sp)]_{+}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\Bigr\\}\\,.",
        "h\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}",
        "R_{h}\\coloneqq I-2\\,\\frac{hh^{\\top}}{\\|h\\|^{2}}\\,,\\hskip 20.00003ptR_{0}\\coloneqq I\\,.",
